## 4 StressDream: Steering High-Dimensional Video World Models

To address the challenges of steering video world models, we propose StressDream, which defines a differentiable optimization criterion by combining a semantic objective using Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with a plausibility objective that prevents OOD noise: 𝒞test=𝒞sem+𝒞pla\mathcal{C}^{\text{test}}=\mathcal{C}^{\text{sem}}+\mathcal{C}^{\text{pla}}. The semantic objective uses inference-time text specifications of high-impact outcomes to reason about whether the target events occur in the generated video (Sec. [4.1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#S4.SS1 "4.1 Semantic Objective: Steering towards Target Events with VLM Gradients ‣ 4 StressDream: Steering High-Dimensional Video World Models ‣ StressDream: Steering Video World Models for Robust Policy Evaluation and Improvement")). The plausibility objective preserves the statistical properties of the standard Gaussian noise prior, thereby maintaining the plausibility of WM generations (Sec. [4.2](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#S4.SS2 "4.2 Plausibility Objective: Keeping High-dimensional Noise in the Typical Set ‣ 4 StressDream: Steering High-Dimensional Video World Models ‣ StressDream: Steering Video World Models for Robust Policy Evaluation and Improvement")). Finally, we approximate the noise gradient to avoid full backpropagation through the iterative denoising process, enabling efficient optimization (Sec. [4.3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#S4.SS3 "4.3 Approximating Noise Gradients for Efficient Steering ‣ 4 StressDream: Steering High-Dimensional Video World Models ‣ StressDream: Steering Video World Models for Robust Policy Evaluation and Improvement")).

