### 4.2 Plausibility Objective: Keeping High-dimensional Noise in the Typical Set

Diffusion models are trained to generate samples from noise drawn from a Gaussian prior. When the noise leaves the typical set [[25](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib123), [13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib122), [83](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib3)], where most training-time noise samples lie, the resulting out-of-distribution (OOD) noise can produce implausible videos that are unlikely under the WM distribution or have degraded visual quality. Importantly, in high dimensions, the typical set is not the same as the region of highest probability density: some noise vectors, such as the zero vector, may have high density but are extremely unlikely to be sampled from the Gaussian prior (See Appendix [B.1](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#A2.SS1 "B.1 Regularizing Noise within the Typical Set of High-Dimensional Gaussian Distribution ‣ Appendix B Inference-time Steering of Video World Models ‣ StressDream: Steering Video World Models for Robust Policy Evaluation and Improvement") for more details). Since gradient-based noise optimization can perturb the noise away from this typical set [[95](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib43), [106](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib30), [34](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib20), [96](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib32), [49](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.00267v1#bib.bib27)], we employ a plausibility objective that encourages the noise to remain within the typical set of the Gaussian prior, with a combination of functions of the noise that capture key statistics of high-dimensional Gaussian noise: 𝒞pla​(ϵ)=λ1​𝒞norm​(ϵ)+λ2​𝒞iso​(ϵ)+λ3​𝒞spec​(ϵ)\mathcal{C}^{\text{pla}}(\bm{\epsilon})=\lambda\_{1}\mathcal{C}^{\text{norm}}(\bm{\epsilon})+\lambda\_{2}\mathcal{C}^{\text{iso}}(\bm{\epsilon})+\lambda\_{3}\mathcal{C}^{\text{spec}}(\bm{\epsilon}).

